    <string name="way_note_aosta">Aosta was Augusta Praetoria, a Roman town founded in 25 BC; its Arch of Augustus still stands.</string>
    <string name="way_note_assisi">Assisi is Francis’s town: his tomb lies beneath the basilica that bears his name.</string>
    <string name="way_note_astorga">In Astorga stands the Episcopal Palace designed by Antoni Gaudí.</string>
    <string name="way_note_bologna">The way begins in Piazza Maggiore, the heart of Bologna since the Middle Ages.</string>
    <string name="way_note_bolsena">Bolsena lies on the largest volcanic lake in Europe.</string>
    <string name="way_note_burgos">Burgos cathedral, begun in 1221, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</string>
    <string name="way_note_cruz_de_ferro">At the iron cross, about 1,500 metres up, pilgrims leave a stone brought from home.</string>
    <string name="way_note_fiesole">Fiesole is older than Florence: the Etruscans founded it, and its Roman theatre still holds summer shows.</string>
    <string name="way_note_firenze">The way ends in Piazza della Signoria, in front of Palazzo Vecchio.</string>
    <string name="way_note_gran_san_bernardo">Travellers have found a hospice at this pass since about 1050, when Bernard of Aosta founded it.</string>
    <string name="way_note_gubbio">In Gubbio, the Fioretti tell, Francis tamed the wolf that frightened the town.</string>
    <string name="way_note_ivrea">Ivrea’s carnival is known for its battle of the oranges.</string>
    <string name="way_note_la_verna">Francis received the stigmata at La Verna in 1224, his first biographers wrote.</string>
    <string name="way_note_leon">León cathedral holds nearly 1,800 square metres of medieval stained glass.</string>
    <string name="way_note_lucca">Lucca’s Renaissance walls, over four kilometres around, are a park on top.</string>
    <string name="way_note_madonna_dei_fornelli">Beyond the village the way follows stretches of the Flaminia Militare, a road the Romans opened in 187 BC.</string>
    <string name="way_note_monteriggioni">In the Inferno, Dante likened Monteriggioni’s ring of towers to giants.</string>
    <string name="way_note_o_cebreiro">O Cebreiro, at the gate of Galicia, keeps its round stone houses with thatched roofs, the pallozas.</string>
    <string name="way_note_orio_litta">Near here the way crosses the Po by boat, where Archbishop Sigeric crossed in 990.</string>
    <string name="way_note_pamplona">Every July, for San Fermín, Pamplona’s bulls run through these streets.</string>
    <string name="way_note_passo_della_cisa">At the Cisa Pass, 1,041 metres up, the way crosses the Apennines into Tuscany.</string>
    <string name="way_note_passo_della_futa">At the Futa Pass, on the old Gothic Line, lies a German war cemetery of the Second World War.</string>
    <string name="way_note_pavia">Saint Augustine is buried in Pavia, in the church of San Pietro in Ciel d’Oro.</string>
    <string name="way_note_piediluco">Nearby, the Velino drops into the Marmore Falls, made by the Romans in 271 BC.</string>
    <string name="way_note_poggio_bustone">Poggio Bustone is one of the four Franciscan sanctuaries of the Rieti valley, the Holy Valley.</string>
    <string name="way_note_ponferrada">Ponferrada’s castle was held by the Knights Templar.</string>
    <string name="way_note_puente_la_reina">The town is named after its Romanesque bridge, built for pilgrims in the 11th century.</string>
    <string name="way_note_radicofani">Radicofani’s fortress was held by Ghino di Tacco, the gentleman bandit of the Decameron.</string>
    <string name="way_note_rieti">Rieti calls itself the centre of Italy: a stone in Piazza San Rufo marks the spot.</string>
    <string name="way_note_roma_san_pietro">The way ends in St Peter’s Square, before the basilica.</string>
    <string name="way_note_roncesvalles">Here, in 778, Charlemagne’s rearguard fell: the battle of the Song of Roland.</string>
    <string name="way_note_saint_jean">Many pilgrims set out from here, at the foot of the Pyrenees.</string>
    <string name="way_note_san_gimignano">San Gimignano still has 14 of its medieval towers, out of about 72.</string>
    <string name="way_note_san_luca">The climb to San Luca runs under a portico 3.8 km long, with 666 arches.</string>
    <string name="way_note_san_piero_a_sieve">Above San Piero stands the fortress of San Martino, built by the Medici in the Mugello, their family’s valley.</string>
    <string name="way_note_sansepolcro">Sansepolcro is Piero della Francesca’s town; his Resurrection hangs in its civic museum.</string>
    <string name="way_note_santiago">The way ends in the Praza do Obradoiro, before the cathedral of Santiago.</string>
    <string name="way_note_sarria">From Sarria, a little over 100 km from Santiago, the walk still earns the Compostela.</string>
    <string name="way_note_siena">Siena’s Piazza del Campo holds the Palio twice each summer, on 2 July and 16 August.</string>
    <string name="way_note_spoleto">Spoleto’s Ponte delle Torri, 230 metres long, began as a Roman aqueduct across the gorge.</string>
    <string name="way_note_vercelli">Vercelli keeps a 10th-century book of Old English poems, probably brought by English pilgrims.</string>
    <string name="way_note_viterbo">In Viterbo, from 1268 to 1271, the cardinals held the longest papal election in history.</string>
